# Open-shell response on a DOUBLET reference: fluorine atom, one unpaired
# electron (nopen 1 -> 5 alpha, 4 beta), static and dynamic hyperpolarizability
# requested.
#
# This case pins a GAP, not a number. The response solver is shell-generic and
# every finite-difference leg below converges, but the quadratic source is
# closed-shell only, so the beta nodes stall, are quarantined, and no property
# row is ever assembled -- the run still exits 0. See the open-shell report
# (reports/2026-09-04_report_openshell_lr): "the quadratic source is closed-shell
# only ... what is missing is the per-spin construction of the occupied-space
# relaxation block and the assembly of the source over the alpha and beta blocks".
# Alpha assembly is refused for the same reason (calc_executor.hpp:2323).
#
# When open-shell quadratic response lands, replace the dropped_work and
# stop_reason assertions below with beta_zzz values and regenerate.
#
# Run:   ./run.sh          (or: madqc --wf=response response_f_doublet_beta.in)

dft
    xc              hf
    nopen           1
    spin_restricted false
    econv           1e-4
    dconv           1e-4
    k               6
    protocol        [1e-4]
    l               30.0
    maxiter         30
end

response
    dipole               true
    dipole.directions    z
    dipole.frequencies   [0.0, 0.05]
    quadratic            true
    requested_properties [polarizability, hyperpolarizability]
    dconv                1e-4
    maxiter              30
    print_level          1
end

molecule
    units   atomic
    eprec   1e-6
    F  0.0  0.0  0.0
end

# Fluorine, not lithium: the convergence gate is an ABSOLUTE residual
# (bsh < 5*max(thresh,dconv)), so a system with a large polarizability cannot
# reach it. Li (alpha ~164 au) diverges here even statically, residual ~8;
# F (alpha ~3.7 au, first excitation ~0.47 au) converges every leg, and the
# second-harmonic leg at 0.10 au stays far below the pole.
